well I think you see in the end this is  a question that you know I used to have  to answer this question a lot and it's  it's in the end of sort of curious thing  because I used to say to people well  first of all if I am of religious faith  and I happen to be the Prime Minister  it's a bit weird if I have to kind of  deny it or conceal it right and actually  it's not very you can't really do that  because someone's bound to catch you  going to church one day and so you'll be  out of so and when people talk about  decisions being I think the more  important thing is when they're all  about decisions being made on faith  grounds I mean I always used to say  there's a very clear distinction to me  between religious faith playing its part  in shaping my political character and in  given me a sense of values that of  course I bring to my political  decision-making because that's what  political decisions are about but I  always used to distinguish between that  because if you you're not careful that  slips into certainly in the media world  we live in you know people used to say  this by myself and George Bush over Iraq  it's not a religious decision now the  fact that you are somewhat of religious  faith you may bring certain values to  your decision making but unfortunately  and this is the problem I think I've  said to you before about religious faith  is it doesn't tell you the right answer  in policy terms you can give you your  values but it doesn't tell you whether  it's a good idea or a bad idea you know  I used to say to people if you go into a  corner and pray to God he won't tell you  the what the minimum wage should be next  year it just doesn't it's not like that  if it was governing would be a lot  simpler can assure you you just say I've  have asked and there it is so there's no  point having innovate about it we've  settled that so I I think that what I do  think is though that political leaders  and people used to even I used to say  the reason I don't talk about my faith a  lot in politics the UK's that's not our  tradition let me say gee that was one  reason why  right the other reason I didn't is  because I do think that political  leaders have got to be obedient to the  essential principles of the democracy we  were setting out and I personally would  feel uncomfortable if my leader of my  country was taking decisions I don't be  motivated in the general sense by their  faith because that's what they are as a  person but we're saying you know this is  this is instructions from God that's why  I'm doing it I mean that that would make  me you know I'd sort of them start  building the bunker roundabout then I  think so so I think you you know you  what I would say is you can't ignore the  fact of faith in someone's lives are how  they developed their character but I  think it is incumbent upon political  leaders and positions of authority to be  careful with how they use that and  careful as to how they articulate or  explain it because you can if you're not  careful and collide with what I think  are pretty essential principles of  liberal democracy
 

